# Chapter 10 — Project Structure

## Part 4 — Packages

Gradle modules define **large architectural boundaries**.

Packages define the **internal organization inside those boundaries**.

This distinction becomes extremely important in a KMP project.

A repository can have excellent modules and still become difficult to maintain if every module contains:

```text
utils/
models/
managers/
helpers/
common/
```

without clear ownership.

The opposite problem also exists.

A project can create hundreds of Gradle modules when a simple package would have been enough.

The goal is not:

```text
More packages
```

or:

```text
More modules
```

The goal is:

> **Use modules for meaningful dependency and ownership boundaries, and packages for meaningful code organization inside those boundaries.**

---

# 1. Package vs Module

The first distinction to understand:

```text
Gradle Module
    ↓
Build boundary
Dependency boundary
Ownership boundary
API boundary

Package
    ↓
Code organization boundary
Namespace
Discoverability
Logical grouping
```

A package normally lives inside a module.

```text
Module
└── Package
    ├── Class
    ├── Interface
    └── Function
```

---

# 2. The Basic Mental Model

Think about project structure at multiple levels:

```text
Repository
    │
    ▼
Application / Module
    │
    ▼
Feature
    │
    ▼
Package
    │
    ▼
Class / Function
```

Each level answers a different question.

| Level | Main Question |
|---|---|
| Repository | What belongs to this product? |
| Module | What is an independent dependency/build boundary? |
| Feature | What business capability does this code own? |
| Package | How is code organized inside that boundary? |
| Class | What behavior does this type provide? |
| Function | What operation does this code perform? |

---

# 3. Packages Are Not Architectural Layers by Themselves

A package named:

```text
domain
```

does not automatically create Clean Architecture.

A package named:

```text
data
```

does not automatically enforce dependency direction.

This:

```text
com.example.orders.domain
com.example.orders.data
com.example.orders.presentation
```

is only organization.

Architecture comes from:

```text
Dependency direction
+
Ownership
+
Visibility
+
Module boundaries
+
Rules
```

---

# 4. Package Structure Is a Communication Tool

When a developer opens:

```text
features/orders/
```

the package structure should help answer:

```text
Where is the business rule?
Where is the repository contract?
Where is the API mapping?
Where is the UI state?
Where is the platform adapter?
```

Good package organization reduces navigation time.

---

# 5. A Poor Package Structure

A common legacy structure:

```text
orders/
├── models/
├── utils/
├── managers/
├── helpers/
├── services/
├── repositories/
└── screens/
```

The problem is not that these names are always wrong.

The problem is that they often describe:

```text
Technical type
```

rather than:

```text
Business responsibility
```

---

# 6. Feature-Oriented Packages

A stronger structure is:

```text
orders/
├── domain/
├── data/
├── presentation/
└── navigation/
```

or, for a smaller feature:

```text
orders/
├── Order.kt
├── OrderRepository.kt
├── OrderScreen.kt
└── OrderViewModel.kt
```

The right depth depends on complexity.

---

# 7. Package Depth Should Follow Complexity

Do not create:

```text
orders/
  domain/
    model/
      entity/
        aggregate/
          valueobject/
```

for a feature containing five classes.

Package hierarchy should communicate real boundaries.

---

# 8. The Package Depth Rule

A useful heuristic:

```text
Low complexity
→ flatter packages

High complexity
→ deeper packages
```

Do not introduce hierarchy simply because an architecture diagram contains layers.

---

# 9. Package-by-Feature

One of the strongest defaults for application code is:

```text
features/
├── orders/
├── payments/
├── profile/
└── inventory/
```

Each feature owns its internal packages.

This keeps related code physically close.

---

# 10. Package-by-Layer

Another approach is:

```text
presentation/
├── orders/
├── payments/
└── profile/

domain/
├── orders/
├── payments/
└── profile/

data/
├── orders/
├── payments/
└── profile/
```

This can work.

But it spreads one business capability across the repository.

For large products, this often increases navigation cost.

---

# 11. Feature vs Layer Organization

Compare:

### Layer-first

```text
domain/
└── orders/

data/
└── orders/

presentation/
└── orders/
```

### Feature-first

```text
orders/
├── domain/
├── data/
└── presentation/
```

Feature-first makes:

```text
Business ownership
```

more visible.

---

# 12. KMP Recommendation

For most medium and large KMP applications:

```text
Feature-first
```

is a strong default.

Inside the feature:

```text
domain
data
presentation
platform
navigation
```

can be introduced when complexity justifies them.

---

# 13. Example KMP Feature

```text
features/orders/
├── src/
│   ├── commonMain/
│   │   └── com/example/orders/
│   │       ├── domain/
│   │       ├── data/
│   │       ├── presentation/
│   │       └── navigation/
│   │
│   ├── commonTest/
│   ├── androidMain/
│   └── iosMain/
│
└── build.gradle.kts
```

The module defines the major boundary.

Packages organize the internals.

---

# 14. Package Names in KMP

A typical package:

```kotlin
package com.example.orders.domain
```

or:

```kotlin
package com.example.orders.data
```

The package should communicate:

```text
Product
Feature
Responsibility
```

---

# 15. Package Naming Convention

A common convention:

```text
com.company.product.feature.area
```

For example:

```text
com.acme.shop.orders.domain
com.acme.shop.orders.data
com.acme.shop.orders.presentation
```

The exact company naming standard can vary.

Consistency matters more than a specific string.

---

# 16. Package Names Should Be Stable

Package names are referenced by:

```text
Imports
Tests
Reflection
Generated code
Tooling
Documentation
```

Therefore avoid constantly changing package names.

A package rename is architectural work when the package is widely consumed.

---

# 17. Package Names and Business Language

Prefer:

```text
orders
payments
inventory
```

over:

```text
module1
featureA
misc
common
```

The package name should use the team's domain language.

---

# 18. Package Naming Is Architecture Documentation

Compare:

```text
com.example.app.common.utils
```

with:

```text
com.example.app.orders.domain
```

The second tells a developer much more about ownership.

---

# 19. Package-by-Feature Example

```text
features/
├── orders/
│   ├── domain/
│   ├── data/
│   ├── presentation/
│   └── navigation/
│
├── payments/
│   ├── domain/
│   ├── data/
│   └── presentation/
│
└── profile/
    ├── domain/
    ├── data/
    └── presentation/
```

This structure supports local reasoning.

---

# 20. What Is Local Reasoning?

Suppose you are fixing:

```text
Order cancellation
```

Ideally you should mostly inspect:

```text
features/orders
```

instead of searching:

```text
domain/
data/
presentation/
utils/
managers/
services/
```

across the entire repository.

---

# 21. Package Structure and Developer Experience

Good package organization makes it easy to answer:

```text
Where should this code go?
```

That is one of the most important architecture outcomes.

---

# 22. Package Ownership

Every package should have an implicit answer to:

```text
Who owns this behavior?
```

For:

```text
orders.domain
```

the answer is:

```text
Orders feature
```

For:

```text
core.network
```

the answer is:

```text
Platform / Core team
```

---

# 23. Packages Inside Shared Modules

Consider:

```text
core/network/
└── src/commonMain/
    └── com/example/core/network/
        ├── client/
        ├── config/
        ├── error/
        └── serialization/
```

This is useful when the module has enough complexity.

---

# 24. Packages Should Not Replace Modules

Suppose:

```text
features/orders
features/payments
```

are both packages inside:

```text
:app
```

They look separated.

But they can still directly access each other's internals.

A package does not automatically create a Gradle dependency boundary.

---

# 25. Package vs Module Isolation

```text
Package
    ↓
Logical organization

Module
    ↓
Compiler/build/dependency boundary
```

If you need enforcement:

```text
Use a module.
```

If you only need organization:

```text
Use a package.
```

---

# 26. The Important Question

Ask:

> **Do I need to prevent this code from depending on that code?**

If:

```text
Yes
```

a package may not be enough.

Consider:

```text
Gradle module
```

or architectural validation.

---

# 27. Kotlin `internal`

Kotlin provides another useful boundary:

```kotlin
internal
```

An `internal` declaration is visible within the module.

This means:

```text
Package organization
+
Module visibility
```

can work together.

---

# 28. Package Does Not Equal Visibility

For example:

```kotlin
package com.example.orders.internal

class OrderMapper
```

The package name says:

```text
This is internal.
```

But unless visibility is restricted, other code in the same module can still access it.

For stronger intent:

```kotlin
internal class OrderMapper
```

---

# 29. Package and `internal` Together

A strong pattern:

```text
orders/
├── api/
│   └── OrderRepository.kt
│
└── internal/
    ├── OrderRepositoryImpl.kt
    └── OrderMapper.kt
```

with:

```kotlin
internal class OrderRepositoryImpl
```

The package communicates intent.

`internal` enforces module-level visibility.

---

# 30. Public vs Internal Packages

A useful organization:

```text
orders/
├── api/
├── domain/
├── internal/
└── presentation/
```

But do not create `api/` and `internal/` packages mechanically.

Use them when the public/internal distinction is meaningful.

---

# 31. Public API Package

Suppose a feature exposes:

```kotlin
interface OrdersEntryPoint {
    fun createEntryPoint()
}
```

It may live in:

```text
orders.api
```

while implementation remains:

```text
orders.internal
```

---

# 32. Package-Level API Design

A package can communicate:

```text
This is safe for other parts of the system to use.
```

For example:

```text
orders.api
```

versus:

```text
orders.internal
```

But remember:

> **Naming communicates intent; visibility enforces it.**

---

# 33. Avoid Public Everything

A common problem:

```text
public class
public interface
public data class
```

for every type.

If a class is only used internally:

```kotlin
internal
```

should be considered.

---

# 34. Public API Surface

Suppose a feature contains:

```text
30 classes
```

but only:

```text
3
```

need external access.

The architecture should make that obvious.

```text
Public API
├── OrdersEntryPoint
├── OrdersNavigator
└── OrderSummary
```

Everything else can remain internal.

---

# 35. Package Structure and API Design

Good package structure often mirrors the public API:

```text
orders/
├── api/
│   ├── OrdersEntryPoint.kt
│   └── OrdersNavigator.kt
│
├── domain/
├── data/
└── presentation/
```

The consumer knows where to start.

---

# 36. Package Organization Inside Domain

A domain package might contain:

```text
domain/
├── model/
├── repository/
├── usecase/
└── policy/
```

This can be useful for large features.

But it is not mandatory.

---

# 37. Small Domain Package

For a small feature:

```text
domain/
├── Order.kt
├── OrderRepository.kt
└── CancelOrder.kt
```

This is often easier to navigate.

---

# 38. Large Domain Package

For a large feature:

```text
domain/
├── model/
│   ├── Order.kt
│   └── OrderStatus.kt
│
├── repository/
│   └── OrderRepository.kt
│
├── usecase/
│   ├── GetOrders.kt
│   └── CancelOrder.kt
│
└── policy/
    └── CancellationPolicy.kt
```

Package depth should follow actual complexity.

---

# 39. Package Organization Inside Data

A common structure:

```text
data/
├── remote/
├── local/
├── mapper/
└── repository/
```

For example:

```text
data/
├── remote/
│   ├── OrderApi.kt
│   └── OrderDto.kt
│
├── local/
│   ├── OrderEntity.kt
│   └── OrderDao.kt
│
├── mapper/
│   └── OrderMapper.kt
│
└── repository/
    └── OrderRepositoryImpl.kt
```

---

# 40. Package Organization Inside Presentation

A large Compose feature might use:

```text
presentation/
├── list/
├── details/
├── edit/
└── components/
```

This can be more useful than:

```text
presentation/
├── screens/
├── viewmodels/
├── states/
└── events/
```

because the first groups code by user-facing behavior.

---

# 41. Screen-Oriented Packages

For example:

```text
presentation/
├── order-list/
│   ├── OrderListScreen.kt
│   ├── OrderListViewModel.kt
│   └── OrderListUiState.kt
│
├── order-details/
│   ├── OrderDetailsScreen.kt
│   ├── OrderDetailsViewModel.kt
│   └── OrderDetailsUiState.kt
```

This improves local reasoning.

---

# 42. Package-by-Screen vs Package-by-Type

Compare:

### Type-oriented

```text
screens/
viewmodels/
states/
events/
components/
```

### Behavior-oriented

```text
order-list/
order-details/
order-edit/
```

For complex UI, behavior-oriented packaging often keeps related files together.

---

# 43. Compose and Package Structure

Jetpack Compose makes it easy to create many small components.

Do not create:

```text
components/
```

as a dumping ground.

Instead:

```text
order-details/
├── OrderDetailsScreen.kt
├── OrderDetailsHeader.kt
├── OrderDetailsActions.kt
└── OrderDetailsViewModel.kt
```

when those components belong specifically to the screen.

---

# 44. Shared UI Components

Reusable UI primitives belong in:

```text
design-system/
```

For example:

```text
design-system/
├── button/
├── card/
├── text-field/
└── typography/
```

Feature-specific UI stays in:

```text
features/orders/
```

---

# 45. Package and UI Ownership

```text
Design System
└── AppButton

Orders
└── CancelOrderButton
```

The first is reusable visual infrastructure.

The second represents business meaning.

---

# 46. Package Structure for KMP Source Sets

KMP introduces another dimension:

```text
commonMain
androidMain
iosMain
```

Packages should generally remain semantically aligned across source sets.

Example:

```text
commonMain/
└── com/example/orders/platform/

androidMain/
└── com/example/orders/platform/

iosMain/
└── com/example/orders/platform/
```

---

# 47. Why Consistent Packages Across Source Sets Help

Suppose:

```text
commonMain
com.example.orders.platform.SecureStorage
```

Android:

```text
androidMain
com.example.orders.platform.SecureStorage
```

iOS:

```text
iosMain
com.example.orders.platform.SecureStorage
```

The package structure communicates that these implementations belong to the same capability.

---

# 48. `expect / actual` Package Organization

Example:

```text
orders/
└── platform/
    └── SecureStorage.kt
```

in:

```text
commonMain
```

and matching platform implementations:

```text
androidMain
└── platform/
    └── SecureStorage.kt

iosMain
└── platform/
    └── SecureStorage.kt
```

---

# 49. Platform Package Naming

Avoid:

```text
androidStuff
iosStuff
```

as broad package names.

Prefer:

```text
platform.storage
platform.notifications
platform.camera
```

The package should describe capability, not merely platform.

---

# 50. KMP Package Mental Model

Think:

```text
commonMain
    ↓
What the application means

androidMain / iosMain
    ↓
How the platform provides it
```

Package structure should reinforce this separation.

---

# 51. Common Package Across Platforms

A strong example:

```text
com.example.core.security
```

exists across:

```text
commonMain
androidMain
iosMain
```

This makes the conceptual boundary stable.

---

# 52. Package Naming and Platform Details

Sometimes platform-specific details genuinely need separate packages:

```text
com.example.core.security.android
com.example.core.security.ios
```

Use this only when it improves discoverability.

Do not duplicate platform names everywhere simply because source sets already separate the code.

---

# 53. Packages and `expect / actual`

The common API:

```kotlin
package com.example.core.security

expect class SecureStorage
```

Android:

```kotlin
package com.example.core.security

actual class SecureStorage
```

This keeps imports stable.

---

# 54. Package Organization for `actual`

Avoid:

```text
common.security
android.security
ios.security
```

if it forces consumers to import platform-specific packages.

Prefer the same conceptual package where practical.

---

# 55. Package Structure and Dependency Direction

Suppose:

```text
orders.domain
```

should not depend on:

```text
orders.presentation
```

A package name does not enforce this.

You need:

```text
Module boundaries
Visibility
Architecture tests
Code review
```

---

# 56. Package-Level Dependency Graph

Inside a feature:

```text
presentation
     ↓
domain
     ↑
data
```

The desired relationship may be:

```text
presentation → domain
data → domain
```

while:

```text
domain → presentation
```

should be forbidden.

---

# 57. Package-Level Architecture

```text
features/orders
│
├── presentation ──────┐
│                      │
├── data ──────────────┼──→ domain
│                      │
└── domain              │
                       │
                 business rules
```

The package layout makes the intended architecture visible.

---

# 58. Package vs Dependency Enforcement

A package can communicate:

```text
domain
```

but Kotlin will not automatically stop:

```text
domain → presentation
```

Therefore:

> **Packages explain architecture. Modules and automated rules help enforce it.**

---

# 59. Architecture Tests for Packages

For larger projects, tools can validate package dependencies.

Conceptually:

```text
orders.domain
    ✗
orders.presentation

orders.domain
    ✗
android.*
```

This protects the package architecture.

---

# 60. Package and Platform API Leakage

Bad:

```kotlin
package com.example.orders.domain

import android.content.Context
```

The package says:

```text
domain
```

but the implementation violates the boundary.

The package structure must be backed by dependency rules.

---

# 61. Package and KMP Portability

A good `commonMain` package should avoid:

```text
Android SDK
iOS UIKit
platform-specific APIs
```

unless accessed through an intentional abstraction.

---

# 62. Package Organization for Shared Core

Example:

```text
core/network/
└── src/commonMain/
    └── com/example/core/network/
        ├── client/
        ├── config/
        ├── error/
        └── interceptor/
```

Platform-specific engine setup:

```text
androidMain/
└── com/example/core/network/engine/

iosMain/
└── com/example/core/network/engine/
```

---

# 63. Package Organization for Security

```text
core/security/
└── src/commonMain/
    └── com/example/core/security/
        ├── auth/
        ├── storage/
        └── crypto/
```

The exact breakdown depends on complexity.

---

# 64. Avoid Over-Packaging

A common mistake:

```text
security/
├── auth/
│   ├── contract/
│   └── implementation/
├── storage/
│   ├── contract/
│   └── implementation/
└── crypto/
    ├── contract/
    └── implementation/
```

for a module containing ten files.

This creates navigation overhead.

---

# 65. The Package Density Rule

If a package contains only:

```text
1–2 classes
```

and has no meaningful semantic boundary, ask whether the extra package is necessary.

Do not optimize for package count.

---

# 66. Empty or Nearly Empty Packages

Avoid creating package directories because:

```text
"Maybe we will need them later."
```

Create structure as the code earns it.

---

# 67. Package Growth

A package can evolve:

```text
orders.domain
```

then:

```text
orders.domain.model
orders.domain.policy
orders.domain.repository
```

when the number of concepts justifies the split.

This is normal architecture evolution.

---

# 68. Package Refactoring

Package refactoring is often cheaper than module refactoring.

For example:

```text
orders.models
```

can become:

```text
orders.domain.model
orders.data.remote
```

without necessarily creating new Gradle modules.

---

# 69. Package Extraction as an Intermediate Step

A useful migration path:

```text
Monolithic package
        ↓
Feature packages
        ↓
Clear internal boundaries
        ↓
Extract Gradle modules when needed
```

Packages can therefore be an intermediate architectural tool.

---

# 70. Package Structure in a Monolith

Even a single KMP module can be organized well:

```text
shared/
└── src/commonMain/
    └── com/example/app/
        ├── orders/
        ├── payments/
        ├── profile/
        └── core/
```

This is much better than:

```text
com/example/app/
├── models/
├── utils/
└── managers/
```

---

# 71. Package Structure Before Modularization

A monolithic KMP project can prepare for future modules:

```text
app/
├── orders/
├── payments/
└── profile/
```

Later:

```text
:features:orders
:features:payments
:features:profile
```

The package structure already communicates ownership.

---

# 72. Package-to-Module Migration

For example:

```text
app
└── com.example.orders
```

can move to:

```text
features/orders
└── com.example.orders
```

The package name can remain stable.

This minimizes import churn.

---

# 73. Stable Package Names During Module Extraction

This is a useful migration technique:

```text
Before:
:app
└── com.example.orders

After:
:features:orders
└── com.example.orders
```

The module boundary changes.

The package namespace does not necessarily need to change.

---

# 74. Package Structure and Refactoring Cost

Good package naming reduces future refactoring because:

```text
Business ownership
```

is already visible.

Bad package organization forces:

```text
File moves
Imports
Visibility changes
Tests
Documentation
```

during every modularization effort.

---

# 75. Package Naming and Domain-Driven Design

Domain-driven design encourages language such as:

```text
orders
payments
inventory
identity
```

rather than:

```text
manager
helper
service
processor
```

when the business concept is more meaningful.

---

# 76. Package by Business Capability

Instead of:

```text
services/
└── OrderService.kt
```

consider:

```text
orders/
└── domain/
    └── OrderService.kt
```

if the service is actually an Orders business capability.

Better still, name the behavior precisely:

```text
CancelOrder.kt
```

when that is what the class actually does.

---

# 77. Avoid Generic Class Names

Packages cannot fully compensate for vague classes.

Bad:

```text
Manager
Helper
Processor
Handler
Util
Service
```

when the actual behavior is known.

Prefer:

```text
OrderCancellationPolicy
PaymentAuthorizer
CustomerMapper
InventorySynchronizer
```

---

# 78. Package + Class Naming

Good:

```text
orders/
└── domain/
    └── policy/
        └── OrderCancellationPolicy.kt
```

The package and class name reinforce each other.

---

# 79. Package Structure and Searchability

Developers frequently navigate code using:

```text
IDE search
Go to symbol
Find usages
Package tree
```

Semantic package names make all of these workflows faster.

---

# 80. Package and IDE Navigation

Compare:

```text
utils/
```

with:

```text
orders/domain/policy/
```

When searching for cancellation behavior, the second gives immediate context.

---

# 81. Package Structure and Code Review

A reviewer should be able to identify:

```text
Is this business logic?
Is this infrastructure?
Is this UI?
Is this platform code?
```

from the location.

That reduces review ambiguity.

---

# 82. Package Structure and Code Ownership

Packages can map naturally to:

```text
CODEOWNERS
```

or team ownership conventions.

For example:

```text
/features/orders/
```

can belong to:

```text
Orders Team
```

while:

```text
/core/security/
```

belongs to:

```text
Security/Platform Team
```

---

# 83. Package Ownership and Teams

Package structure can therefore align:

```text
Business capability
        ↓
Package
        ↓
Module
        ↓
Team
```

This is a powerful enterprise architecture pattern.

---

# 84. Package and Team Boundaries

However:

```text
Package ≠ team boundary
```

A package can be too small to justify independent ownership.

Use modules for stronger boundaries.

---

# 85. Package Structure and CODEOWNERS

Example:

```text
features/orders/
    → Orders Team

core/security/
    → Platform Security Team

design-system/
    → Design System Team
```

This makes ownership explicit.

---

# 86. Package Organization for APIs

Feature API package:

```text
orders/api/
├── OrdersEntryPoint.kt
└── OrdersRoute.kt
```

Internal packages:

```text
orders/domain/
orders/data/
orders/presentation/
```

This is especially useful when other modules consume a feature entry point.

---

# 87. Package Organization for Contracts

A contracts module may use:

```text
contracts/payment/
├── PaymentGateway.kt
├── PaymentRequest.kt
└── PaymentResult.kt
```

Keep contracts small and stable.

---

# 88. Package Structure and Shared Contracts

Avoid:

```text
contracts/
└── models/
    └── everything.kt
```

Contracts should represent specific capabilities.

---

# 89. Package Structure for Integration Adapters

For vendor integrations:

```text
integrations/
├── analytics/
│   ├── AnalyticsAdapter.kt
│   └── FirebaseAnalyticsAdapter.kt
│
└── payments/
    ├── PaymentGatewayAdapter.kt
    └── StripePaymentAdapter.kt
```

The package makes the integration boundary visible.

---

# 90. Package Structure and Vendor Leakage

Consumers should ideally import:

```kotlin
import com.example.analytics.Analytics
```

not:

```kotlin
import com.vendor.sdk.FirebaseAnalytics
```

The package structure should help prevent vendor leakage.

---

# 91. Package Structure and Repository Implementations

Inside a feature:

```text
orders/
├── domain/
│   └── repository/
│       └── OrderRepository.kt
│
└── data/
    └── repository/
        └── OrderRepositoryImpl.kt
```

This makes the dependency inversion visible.

---

# 92. Repository Contract Ownership

The repository interface belongs close to the business abstraction:

```text
domain.repository
```

The implementation belongs to:

```text
data.repository
```

The package structure communicates the distinction.

---

# 93. Package Structure and Mappers

Mappers can become another dumping ground.

Avoid:

```text
mappers/
├── OrderMapper
├── PaymentMapper
├── ProfileMapper
└── EverythingMapper
```

Prefer placing mapping close to its context:

```text
orders/data/mapper/
payments/data/mapper/
profile/data/mapper/
```

---

# 94. Package Structure and DTOs

DTOs usually belong near the external data source:

```text
orders/data/remote/
└── OrderDto.kt
```

rather than:

```text
shared/models/
└── OrderDto.kt
```

This protects feature boundaries.

---

# 95. Package Structure and Database Entities

Similarly:

```text
orders/data/local/
└── OrderEntity.kt
```

rather than:

```text
core/database/entities/
└── OrderEntity.kt
```

if the entity belongs to Orders.

---

# 96. Package Structure and Mapping Boundary

A healthy flow:

```text
Remote DTO
    ↓
Mapper
    ↓
Domain Model
    ↓
UI Model
```

Packages can make each boundary visible.

---

# 97. Package Structure and UI Models

Avoid putting:

```text
OrderUiState
```

inside:

```text
domain/model
```

when it represents presentation concerns.

Prefer:

```text
orders/presentation/order-list/
└── OrderListUiState.kt
```

---

# 98. Package Structure and Domain Models

Domain models should not contain:

```text
Compose state
Android Context
UI-specific strings
Views
```

A package name alone does not enforce this.

But good placement makes the violation obvious.

---

# 99. Package Structure and Platform Code

Platform-specific implementations should remain close to the capability they implement.

Example:

```text
core/security/
├── commonMain/
│   └── SecureStorage.kt
│
├── androidMain/
│   └── SecureStorage.android.kt
│
└── iosMain/
    └── SecureStorage.ios.kt
```

The package remains semantically consistent.

---

# 100. Package Structure and Source Set Trees

A KMP module may look like:

```text
core/security/
├── src/
│   ├── commonMain/
│   │   └── com/example/security/
│   │       ├── SecureStorage.kt
│   │       └── SecurityError.kt
│   │
│   ├── commonTest/
│   │   └── com/example/security/
│   │       └── SecureStorageTest.kt
│   │
│   ├── androidMain/
│   │   └── com/example/security/
│   │       └── SecureStorage.android.kt
│   │
│   └── iosMain/
│       └── com/example/security/
│           └── SecureStorage.ios.kt
```

This makes source-set responsibilities clear.

---

# 101. Test Packages

Keep test package names aligned with production packages.

Production:

```text
com.example.orders.domain
```

Test:

```text
com.example.orders.domain
```

This makes tests easy to locate.

---

# 102. Test Package Organization

Example:

```text
commonTest/
└── com/example/orders/
    ├── domain/
    ├── data/
    └── presentation/
```

The test tree mirrors production semantics.

---

# 103. Test Fixtures and Package Ownership

Feature-specific fixtures:

```text
orders/test-fixtures/
```

or:

```text
orders/commonTest/
```

Generic fixtures:

```text
shared/testing/
```

Do not move everything into shared testing.

---

# 104. Package Structure and Test Visibility

`internal` production APIs can still be tested from within the same module.

This is another reason not to make everything public just for tests.

---

# 105. Package Structure and Generated Code

KMP projects may use:

```text
KSP
Serialization
DI code generation
Database code generation
```

Generated packages should be predictable and should not be mixed with handwritten architecture packages unnecessarily.

---

# 106. Generated Code Boundaries

Prefer:

```text
generated/
```

or tool-defined packages when appropriate.

Do not manually edit generated code.

---

# 107. Package Structure and Serialization

For `kotlinx.serialization`:

```kotlin
@Serializable
data class OrderDto(
    val id: String
)
```

Keep DTOs near their data boundary:

```text
orders.data.remote
```

unless they are intentionally part of a public contract.

---

# 108. Package Structure and Public Serialization Models

If a model is intentionally shared between modules:

```text
contracts.orders
```

may be appropriate.

But then it becomes a public contract and should be treated accordingly.

---

# 109. Package Structure and API Contracts

A public contract should be:

```text
Stable
Documented
Small
Versioned when necessary
```

Package placement should make its public status clear.

---

# 110. Package Organization for Navigation

A feature may expose:

```text
orders/navigation/
├── OrdersDestination.kt
├── OrdersNavigator.kt
└── OrdersEntryPoint.kt
```

Navigation details remain separate from:

```text
orders/domain
```

---

# 111. Navigation and Package Ownership

A navigation route should not force domain code to know:

```text
Compose NavController
```

or:

```text
UIKit navigation
```

Keep platform/UI navigation concerns near presentation/application composition.

---

# 112. Package Structure and Deep Links

Deep-link definitions may live in:

```text
orders/navigation/
```

while parsing infrastructure may live in:

```text
core/navigation/
```

This keeps:

```text
Infrastructure
```

separate from:

```text
Feature destinations
```

---

# 113. Package Structure and Events

Feature events can remain feature-owned:

```text
orders/presentation/order-details/
└── OrderDetailsEvent.kt
```

Cross-feature events should be modeled intentionally.

Do not create:

```text
shared/events/
```

for every event.

---

# 114. Shared Events Trap

A package like:

```text
shared/events
```

often becomes a hidden coupling mechanism.

If:

```text
OrderCreated
```

is consumed by multiple systems, decide whether it is:

```text
Domain event
Integration event
Analytics event
Application event
```

Then place it accordingly.

---

# 115. Package Structure and Domain Events

A genuine shared domain event may live in:

```text
contracts/events/
```

if multiple independent consumers require the contract.

But do not create shared events merely to avoid defining ownership.

---

# 116. Package Structure and State

Avoid:

```text
shared/state/
```

containing every application's state.

Prefer state ownership:

```text
orders/presentation
payments/presentation
profile/presentation
```

Global state should be intentionally modeled.

---

# 117. Package Structure and State Machines

A feature state machine belongs close to the feature:

```text
orders/domain/
└── OrderStateMachine.kt
```

or:

```text
orders/presentation/order-details/
└── OrderDetailsStateMachine.kt
```

depending on what the state represents.

---

# 118. Package Structure and Coroutines

Coroutine utilities may belong in:

```text
core/concurrency/
```

only when there is meaningful shared behavior.

Do not create:

```text
coroutines/utils/
```

for one helper function.

---

# 119. Package Structure and Dispatchers

If you define:

```kotlin
interface AppDispatchers
```

place it according to ownership:

```text
core/concurrency/
```

if it is application infrastructure.

Feature-specific scheduling belongs to the feature.

---

# 120. Package Structure and Time

A shared clock abstraction may live in:

```text
core/time/
```

if multiple modules require deterministic time behavior.

A feature-specific time policy belongs to:

```text
feature/domain
```

---

# 121. Package Structure and Error Models

Infrastructure errors:

```text
core/network/error
```

Feature errors:

```text
orders/domain/error
payments/domain/error
```

This prevents infrastructure concepts from leaking into business layers.

---

# 122. Package Structure and Exception Translation

Example:

```text
NetworkException
      ↓
Repository
      ↓
OrderError
      ↓
ViewModel
      ↓
UI
```

The package boundaries can mirror the translation stages.

---

# 123. Package Structure and Logging

A feature can log through:

```kotlin
logger.error("Order cancellation failed")
```

but the logger implementation remains:

```text
core/observability
```

The feature package owns the message.

The infrastructure package owns the logging mechanism.

---

# 124. Package Structure and Metrics

Generic metrics:

```text
core/observability
```

Feature metrics:

```text
orders/observability
```

when the metrics have feature-specific semantic ownership.

---

# 125. Package Structure and Analytics

Generic analytics capability:

```text
core/analytics
```

Feature event definitions:

```text
orders/analytics
```

This keeps business meaning near its feature.

---

# 126. Package Structure and Feature Flags

Flag infrastructure:

```text
core/featureflags
```

Feature flags:

```text
orders/config
```

or:

```text
orders/featureflags
```

depending on project conventions.

---

# 127. Package Structure and Configuration

Global infrastructure:

```text
core/config
```

Feature configuration:

```text
orders/config
```

Do not put every configuration value into:

```text
GlobalConfig
```

---

# 128. Package Structure and Environment

Environment selection may live in:

```text
app/config
```

or:

```text
core/config
```

while:

```text
OrderConfiguration
```

belongs to Orders if it represents feature-specific policy.

---

# 129. Package Structure and Security

Security package organization can be:

```text
core/security/
├── auth/
├── crypto/
├── storage/
└── biometrics/
```

But only introduce subpackages when each represents a meaningful capability.

---

# 130. Package Structure and Authentication

Authentication feature:

```text
features/authentication/
├── domain/
├── data/
├── presentation/
└── navigation/
```

Security infrastructure:

```text
core/security/
```

The distinction is:

```text
Authentication = product capability
Security = infrastructure capability
```

---

# 131. Package Structure and Authorization

Authorization rules can be:

```text
orders/domain/policy/
```

while identity and credential infrastructure lives in:

```text
core/security/
```

This preserves business ownership.

---

# 132. Package Structure and Offline-First

A feature might use:

```text
orders/data/
├── local/
├── remote/
├── sync/
└── repository/
```

The shared infrastructure might provide:

```text
core/database
core/network
core/sync
```

The feature owns the semantics.

---

# 133. Package Structure and Synchronization

Generic synchronization infrastructure:

```text
core/sync
```

Feature synchronization policy:

```text
orders/data/sync
```

Do not put:

```text
OrderSyncPolicy
```

inside:

```text
core/sync
```

unless it is truly generic.

---

# 134. Package Structure and Background Work

Generic background infrastructure:

```text
core/background
```

Feature work:

```text
orders/background
```

or:

```text
orders/data/sync
```

depending on architecture.

The package should communicate ownership.

---

# 135. Package Structure and Platform Background Work

Android and iOS may implement background work differently.

A shared contract:

```text
BackgroundTaskScheduler
```

can live in:

```text
core/background
```

while platform implementations remain in:

```text
androidMain
iosMain
```

---

# 136. Package Structure and Notifications

Generic:

```text
core/notifications
```

Feature-specific:

```text
orders/notifications
```

For example:

```text
OrderReadyNotification
```

belongs to Orders.

The platform notification mechanism belongs to infrastructure.

---

# 137. Package Structure and Device APIs

Generic capabilities:

```text
platform/camera
platform/location
platform/biometrics
```

should expose platform-neutral contracts.

Business packages consume those capabilities.

---

# 138. Package Structure and Device Leakage

Avoid:

```text
orders/domain
└── AndroidCameraManager.kt
```

This violates both:

```text
domain boundary
```

and:

```text
KMP portability
```

---

# 139. Package Structure and Platform Adapters

Prefer:

```text
platform/camera/
├── commonMain/
│   └── Camera.kt
├── androidMain/
│   └── AndroidCamera.kt
└── iosMain/
    └── IosCamera.kt
```

The feature sees the capability, not the platform details.

---

# 140. Package Structure and Dependency Injection

DI modules can be organized by ownership:

```text
orders/di/
payments/di/
core/network/di/
core/security/di/
```

Avoid a single:

```text
di/
```

package containing every binding in a large application.

---

# 141. DI Package Ownership

Feature DI:

```text
Orders dependencies
```

Core DI:

```text
Network
Database
Security
```

Application composition:

```text
App-level assembly
```

The package tree should reflect this.

---

# 142. Package Structure and Composition Root

A common structure:

```text
app/
└── di/
    └── AppGraph.kt
```

while:

```text
features/orders/di/
```

contains feature-specific bindings.

The composition root connects them.

---

# 143. Package Structure and Dependency Injection Leakage

Avoid putting business decisions inside DI modules:

```kotlin
single {
    if (flags.isEnabled(...)) {
        NewOrderPolicy()
    } else {
        OldOrderPolicy()
    }
}
```

when the feature should own the policy decision.

DI should assemble dependencies, not become a hidden business layer.

---

# 144. Package Structure and Factory Classes

A generic:

```text
Factory
```

package can become vague.

Prefer:

```text
orders/domain/OrderFactory
```

if it creates Orders.

Use semantic names.

---

# 145. Package Structure and Repository Names

Avoid:

```text
repositories/
    BaseRepository.kt
```

unless a meaningful shared abstraction exists.

Prefer:

```text
orders/domain/repository/OrderRepository.kt
```

---

# 146. Package Structure and Use Cases

For small features:

```text
orders/domain/
├── GetOrders.kt
├── CancelOrder.kt
└── CreateOrder.kt
```

For large features:

```text
orders/domain/usecase/
├── GetOrders.kt
├── CancelOrder.kt
└── CreateOrder.kt
```

Again:

```text
Complexity determines depth.
```

---

# 147. Package Structure and One-Class Packages

Avoid:

```text
orders/domain/cancel/
└── CancelOrder.kt
```

unless `cancel` is a substantial subdomain.

One-class packages add navigation cost without meaningful boundaries.

---

# 148. Package Structure and Subdomains

A package can be useful when a capability becomes a subdomain:

```text
orders/
├── fulfillment/
├── cancellation/
├── pricing/
└── tracking/
```

This is valuable when each area contains multiple related concepts.

---

# 149. Package Structure and Bounded Contexts

If:

```text
Orders
```

contains distinct subdomains:

```text
Pricing
Fulfillment
Tracking
```

packages can make those boundaries visible before they become separate modules.

---

# 150. Package Structure and Future Module Extraction

A useful strategy:

```text
orders/
├── pricing/
├── fulfillment/
└── tracking/
```

Later:

```text
:features:orders-pricing
:features:orders-fulfillment
:features:orders-tracking
```

when independent lifecycle or ownership requires it.

---

# 151. Package Structure and Premature Modules

Do not create:

```text
:orders:pricing
```

just because:

```text
pricing/
```

exists.

A package may be sufficient.

Extract a module when:

```text
Dependency isolation
Build isolation
Ownership
Reuse
API encapsulation
```

justify it.

---

# 152. Package Structure and Package Stability

A package that changes together should stay together.

A package that changes independently may deserve separation.

This gives a useful heuristic:

> **Organize code around change patterns, not only around class types.**

---

# 153. Package Structure and Cohesion

High cohesion:

```text
orders/cancellation/
├── CancellationPolicy
├── CancellationReason
└── CancelOrder
```

These classes change for related reasons.

Low cohesion:

```text
utils/
├── DateFormatter
├── PaymentHelper
├── ImageLoader
└── OrderParser
```

They have unrelated reasons to change.

---

# 154. Package Structure and Coupling

A package with many dependencies on unrelated areas may indicate poor cohesion.

For example:

```text
common/
```

depends on:

```text
orders
payments
profile
inventory
```

This should trigger review.

---

# 155. Package Structure and Change Coupling

If changing:

```text
Payment
```

requires touching:

```text
orders/common
profile/common
shared/common
```

the package structure is hiding ownership.

Move code closer to the concept it belongs to.

---

# 156. Package Structure and "Manager" Packages

Avoid:

```text
managers/
```

containing:

```text
OrderManager
PaymentManager
UserManager
SyncManager
```

The package does not communicate why these classes belong together.

Group by business capability or infrastructure responsibility instead.

---

# 157. Package Structure and "Services"

Similarly:

```text
services/
```

can become a dumping ground.

Instead:

```text
orders/
└── domain/
    └── OrderPricingService.kt
```

if it is genuinely an Orders domain service.

---

# 158. Package Structure and "Helpers"

Avoid:

```text
helpers/
```

for miscellaneous logic.

A helper should have a semantic home.

For example:

```text
OrderNumberGenerator
```

belongs near Orders if it is an Orders concept.

---

# 159. Package Structure and Generic Names

A useful test:

> If you cannot explain why two classes belong in the same package, they probably should not be there.

---

# 160. Package Structure and Semantic Cohesion

Good package:

```text
orders/cancellation/
```

contains concepts related to:

```text
Cancellation
```

Bad package:

```text
common/
```

contains anything that is:

```text
Used more than once
```

---

# 161. Package Structure and Reuse

Reuse should happen through:

```text
Intentional shared capability
```

not:

```text
Accidental proximity
```

If code becomes reusable, extract it into a meaningful shared module or package when appropriate.

---

# 162. Package Structure and Shared Utilities

For example:

```text
formatCurrency()
```

might live in:

```text
core/localization
```

if it has broad semantic meaning.

But:

```text
formatOrderPrice()
```

belongs to:

```text
orders
```

---

# 163. Package Structure and API Design

Package names can guide API usage:

```text
core.security.SecureStorage
```

communicates:

```text
Capability
```

while:

```text
core.common.BaseManager
```

communicates almost nothing.

---

# 164. Package Structure and Domain Language

Use nouns from the product:

```text
orders
payments
inventory
customers
subscriptions
```

This makes the repository readable to:

```text
Engineers
Product Managers
Architects
New Team Members
```

---

# 165. Package Structure and Team Communication

When someone says:

> “The issue is in `orders.fulfillment`.”

the package name becomes part of the team's language.

This is valuable in large organizations.

---

# 166. Package Structure and Documentation

Documentation can reference package boundaries:

```text
Order cancellation logic:
features/orders/domain/cancellation

Payment integration:
features/payments/data/gateway
```

This makes architecture documentation concrete.

---

# 167. Package Structure and Git History

Meaningful packages also improve Git history interpretation.

Instead of:

```text
common/Helper.kt
```

developers see:

```text
orders/cancellation/CancellationPolicy.kt
```

The intent is clearer.

---

# 168. Package Structure and Code Ownership

When a file changes:

```text
features/orders/domain/cancellation/
```

the owner is obvious.

This improves:

```text
Code review routing
On-call ownership
Incident investigation
```

---

# 169. Package Structure and Incident Response

Suppose production reports:

```text
Payment authorization failures
```

A semantic package structure points toward:

```text
features/payments
integrations/payments
core/network
core/observability
```

rather than a generic:

```text
services/
```

---

# 170. Package Structure and Debugging

Good package boundaries reduce search space.

```text
Bug
 ↓
Capability
 ↓
Feature
 ↓
Package
 ↓
Implementation
```

This is much faster than searching the entire codebase.

---

# 171. Package Structure and IDE Refactoring

Semantic packages help IDE refactoring because related code is grouped.

For example:

```text
orders/fulfillment
```

can be moved or extracted as a unit when necessary.

---

# 172. Package Structure and Module Extraction

A mature feature can evolve:

```text
orders/
├── domain/
├── data/
├── presentation/
└── fulfillment/
```

Later:

```text
features/orders
features/order-fulfillment
```

The package boundary provided the initial semantic separation.

---

# 173. Package Structure and Architecture Evolution

Architecture should evolve gradually:

```text
Package
   ↓
Module
   ↓
Library
   ↓
Published capability
```

Do not jump directly to the most expensive boundary.

---

# 174. Package Structure and KMP Architecture

A KMP architecture can therefore be visualized as:

```text
Repository
   │
   ├── Applications
   │
   ├── Feature Modules
   │      │
   │      └── Packages
   │
   ├── Shared Modules
   │      │
   │      └── Packages
   │
   ├── Platform Modules
   │      │
   │      └── Packages
   │
   └── Build Logic
```

Packages provide the internal map.

---

# 175. Package Structure and Source Sets

The full hierarchy can be:

```text
Module
  │
  ├── commonMain
  │      └── package
  │
  ├── commonTest
  │      └── package
  │
  ├── androidMain
  │      └── package
  │
  └── iosMain
         └── package
```

Each source set has its own implementation scope.

---

# 176. Package Structure and Common Code

A common package:

```text
com.example.orders.domain
```

should contain code that is truly common.

If it contains Android APIs:

```text
commonMain
```

is no longer portable.

---

# 177. Package Structure and Platform Code

Android:

```text
com.example.orders.platform.android
```

can contain:

```text
Android-specific implementation
```

but often the better pattern is to keep the conceptual package stable:

```text
com.example.orders.platform
```

across source sets.

---

# 178. Package Structure and iOS Code

Likewise:

```text
com.example.orders.platform
```

can contain:

```text
IosSecureStorage
```

in `iosMain`.

The source set already communicates the platform.

---

# 179. Package Structure and Platform Naming

Because:

```text
androidMain
```

already tells you:

```text
Android
```

you do not always need:

```text
.android
```

in every package.

Avoid redundant naming.

---

# 180. Package Structure and Multiplatform Hierarchy

With intermediate source sets:

```text
commonMain
    │
    ├── appleMain
    │       ├── iosMain
    │       └── macosMain
    │
    └── androidMain
```

packages can remain aligned with the capability.

---

# 181. Package Structure and Apple Shared Code

For example:

```text
core/security
└── appleMain
    └── com/example/security/
        └── AppleSecureStorage.kt
```

if the implementation is genuinely shared by Apple targets.

---

# 182. Package Structure and Package Stability Across Platforms

A stable package namespace makes platform implementations easier to locate:

```text
com.example.core.security
```

regardless of:

```text
Android
iOS
macOS
```

---

# 183. Package Structure and Kotlin Naming

Use standard Kotlin naming:

```text
lowercase.package.names
```

Classes:

```text
PascalCase
```

Functions:

```text
camelCase
```

Avoid:

```text
Com.Example.Orders
```

or inconsistent package capitalization.

---

# 184. Package Structure and File Names

A file should generally communicate its primary concept:

```text
OrderRepository.kt
CancelOrder.kt
OrderMapper.kt
OrderDetailsScreen.kt
```

Avoid:

```text
Common.kt
Utils.kt
Helper.kt
Stuff.kt
```

unless the purpose is genuinely broad and well-defined.

---

# 185. Package Structure and Top-Level Functions

Kotlin allows top-level functions.

This is useful for focused behavior:

```kotlin
package com.example.orders.domain.pricing

fun calculateOrderTotal(...)
```

But do not use top-level functions to create an unstructured utility package.

The package must provide semantic ownership.

---

# 186. Package Structure and Extension Functions

Extension functions should live near the concept they extend.

For example:

```kotlin
package com.example.orders.data.mapper

fun OrderDto.toDomain(): Order
```

This is clearer than:

```text
common/extensions
```

containing hundreds of unrelated extensions.

---

# 187. Extension Function Dumping Grounds

Avoid:

```text
extensions/
├── StringExtensions.kt
├── ListExtensions.kt
├── OrderExtensions.kt
└── EverythingExtensions.kt
```

Organize by ownership.

---

# 188. Package Structure and Kotlin Serialization Extensions

For example:

```kotlin
orders.data.remote.mapper
```

can contain:

```kotlin
fun OrderDto.toDomain()
```

while:

```text
domain
```

remains unaware of transport DTOs.

---

# 189. Package Structure and Compose Extensions

Feature-specific Compose helpers:

```text
orders.presentation.order-details
```

Shared design-system helpers:

```text
design-system
```

Do not put all Compose extensions into:

```text
ui.extensions
```

---

# 190. Package Structure and Resources

In Compose Multiplatform projects, resources may be organized by:

```text
feature
```

when the tooling and project architecture support it.

The conceptual ownership should remain:

```text
Orders resources
Payments resources
Design system resources
```

rather than:

```text
Global resource dumping ground
```

---

# 191. Package Structure and Localization Resources

Feature-specific strings should remain discoverable near the feature.

Shared design-system resources belong to the design system.

This reduces accidental cross-feature coupling.

---

# 192. Package Structure and Serialization Models

Transport models:

```text
orders.data.remote.model
```

Database models:

```text
orders.data.local.model
```

Domain models:

```text
orders.domain.model
```

UI models:

```text
orders.presentation.model
```

Each model has a clear reason to exist.

---

# 193. Package Structure and Model Duplication

This may create:

```text
OrderDto
OrderEntity
Order
OrderUiModel
```

Some developers see this as unnecessary duplication.

But each model represents a different boundary.

Duplication can protect:

```text
Domain independence
API evolution
Database evolution
UI evolution
```

---

# 194. Package Structure and Model Mapping

```text
OrderDto
   │
   ▼
OrderMapper
   │
   ▼
Order
   │
   ▼
OrderUiModel
```

Packages make these transformations explicit.

---

# 195. Package Structure and UI State

For Compose:

```kotlin
data class OrderDetailsUiState(
    val isLoading: Boolean,
    val order: OrderUiModel?,
    val error: String?
)
```

Place it near:

```text
OrderDetailsScreen
```

when it is screen-specific.

---

# 196. Package Structure and State Reuse

If multiple screens genuinely share the same state abstraction:

```text
orders/presentation/shared
```

may be appropriate.

But avoid:

```text
shared/state
```

as a default.

---

# 197. Package Structure and Presentation Components

A component belongs to the closest meaningful scope.

```text
PrimaryButton
→ Design System

OrderStatusChip
→ Orders

OrderDetailsHeader
→ Order Details screen
```

This prevents the `components` package from becoming a dumping ground.

---

# 198. Package Structure and Component Extraction

When a component is reused:

```text
OrderDetailsHeader
```

should not automatically move to:

```text
shared/components
```

Ask:

```text
Is it reusable across business contexts?
```

If yes:

```text
Design System / Shared UI
```

If no:

```text
Keep feature-local.
```

---

# 199. Package Structure and Domain Services

A domain service can be meaningful:

```text
orders.domain.pricing.OrderPricingService
```

when the behavior does not naturally belong to one entity.

But:

```text
services/
```

with dozens of services is usually a warning sign.

---

# 200. Package Structure and Use-Case Naming

Prefer:

```text
CancelOrder
CreateOrder
GetOrderDetails
TrackOrder
```

over:

```text
OrderManager
OrderService
OrderProcessor
```

when the operation is clear.

---

# 201. Package Structure and Command/Query Concepts

Packages can reflect behavior:

```text
orders/domain/command
orders/domain/query
```

but introduce this only if the distinction is useful.

Do not create package hierarchies because a pattern is fashionable.

---

# 202. Package Structure and CQRS

If a project genuinely uses CQRS:

```text
orders/domain/
├── command/
└── query/
```

can communicate the architecture.

Otherwise:

```text
usecase/
```

may be simpler.

---

# 203. Package Structure and State Machines

If a feature contains complex state transitions:

```text
orders/domain/state/
```

can be meaningful.

For example:

```text
Pending
Confirmed
Packed
Shipped
Delivered
Cancelled
```

This represents a real subdomain.

---

# 204. Package Structure and Policies

Policies can live in:

```text
orders/domain/policy/
```

when there are multiple related policies:

```text
CancellationPolicy
RefundPolicy
ShippingPolicy
```

A single policy does not require a package.

---

# 205. Package Structure and Subdomain Boundaries

A package becomes especially valuable when:

```text
Many related classes
+
Same business concept
+
Independent evolution
```

exist.

That is semantic cohesion.

---

# 206. Package Structure and Architecture Smell: `common`

```text
common/
```

is often a signal that ownership has not been decided.

Ask:

```text
Common to whom?
Why?
Who owns it?
```

Then rename based on the answer.

---

# 207. Architecture Smell: `utils`

```text
utils/
```

often means:

```text
No semantic home.
```

Move each class/function to:

```text
Feature
Core capability
Platform
Design system
```

as appropriate.

---

# 208. Architecture Smell: `helpers`

Same problem:

```text
helpers/
```

is usually too generic.

Name the capability instead.

---

# 209. Architecture Smell: `managers`

Managers frequently hide multiple responsibilities.

For example:

```text
OrderManager
```

may contain:

```text
Fetching
Caching
Validation
Mapping
State
Navigation
Analytics
```

Split responsibilities and place them into meaningful packages.

---

# 210. Architecture Smell: `services`

A generic services package can hide business boundaries.

Ask:

```text
What does this service actually own?
```

Then move it accordingly.

---

# 211. Architecture Smell: `models`

Global:

```text
models/
```

often becomes a model dumping ground.

Prefer:

```text
orders/domain/model
orders/data/remote/model
payments/domain/model
```

or an intentionally shared contract module.

---

# 212. Architecture Smell: `screens`

Global:

```text
screens/
```

can separate UI from its feature.

Prefer:

```text
orders/presentation/order-details
payments/presentation/payment-history
```

when feature ownership matters.

---

# 213. Architecture Smell: `viewmodels`

Global:

```text
viewmodels/
```

has the same problem.

Keep ViewModels near the screen/feature they own.

---

# 214. Architecture Smell: `repositories`

Global:

```text
repositories/
```

hides feature ownership.

Prefer:

```text
orders/domain/repository
payments/domain/repository
```

and implementations inside the respective data boundaries.

---

# 215. Architecture Smell: `mappers`

Global:

```text
mappers/
```

can become enormous.

Keep mappings near their source and target boundaries.

---

# 216. Architecture Smell: `extensions`

Global:

```text
extensions/
```

often becomes a miscellaneous code bucket.

Organize extensions by the concept they belong to.

---

# 217. Architecture Smell: `constants`

Global:

```text
constants/
```

is another common dumping ground.

Ask what each constant represents.

Examples:

```text
NetworkConstants
SecurityConstants
OrdersConstants
```

may be better.

---

# 218. Constants and Ownership

For example:

```kotlin
const val MAX_ORDER_ITEMS = 50
```

belongs to:

```text
orders/domain
```

if it is a business rule.

It should not be:

```text
core/constants
```

just because it is a constant.

---

# 219. Architecture Smell: `enums`

Global:

```text
enums/
```

can hide business concepts.

Prefer:

```text
orders/domain/model/OrderStatus.kt
```

rather than:

```text
common/enums/OrderStatus.kt
```

---

# 220. Package Structure and Sealed Classes

A sealed hierarchy often belongs close to its concept.

For example:

```kotlin
sealed interface OrderError {
    data object Network : OrderError
    data object Unauthorized : OrderError
    data object CancelNotAllowed : OrderError
}
```

Place it near the Orders domain.

---

# 221. Package Structure and Error Hierarchies

If errors are infrastructure-level:

```text
core/network/error
```

If errors are business-level:

```text
orders/domain/error
```

This keeps semantic ownership clear.

---

# 222. Package Structure and Result Types

A generic:

```text
Result
```

may be unnecessary if Kotlin already provides what you need.

Do not create:

```text
common/result/
```

without a strong reason.

---

# 223. Package Structure and Generic Abstractions

The same rule applies to:

```text
BaseResult
BaseState
BaseRepository
BaseUseCase
BaseViewModel
```

Generic abstractions often create coupling without providing enough value.

---

# 224. Package Structure and Inheritance

Packages can hide inheritance coupling.

For example:

```text
common/base/
└── BaseViewModel.kt
```

then:

```text
orders
payments
profile
```

all inherit from it.

Changes to the base class now affect every feature.

Composition may be better.

---

# 225. Package Structure and Composition

Prefer:

```text
orders.presentation
    ↓
Logger
Analytics
DispatcherProvider
```

over:

```text
BaseViewModel
```

when dependencies can be explicit.

---

# 226. Package Structure and Dependency Injection

Explicit dependencies improve package reasoning:

```kotlin
class OrderViewModel(
    private val getOrders: GetOrders,
    private val logger: Logger
)
```

The package tells you:

```text
Orders presentation
```

The constructor tells you:

```text
Required capabilities
```

---

# 227. Package Structure and Hidden Dependencies

Avoid service locators:

```kotlin
ServiceLocator.get<OrderRepository>()
```

because package structure cannot reveal the dependency.

Explicit injection keeps architecture visible.

---

# 228. Package Structure and Circular Imports

Kotlin package names do not inherently prevent cycles.

Example:

```text
orders.presentation
    ↓
payments.domain
    ↓
orders.domain
```

This may still compile.

Module boundaries and architecture rules are needed for stronger enforcement.

---

# 229. Package Structure and Feature-to-Feature Communication

Avoid importing internal classes:

```kotlin
import com.example.orders.internal.OrderRepositoryImpl
```

from Payments.

If Payments needs something from Orders, define an intentional contract.

---

# 230. Package Structure and Public Feature APIs

For example:

```text
orders/api/OrdersEntryPoint.kt
```

can provide:

```kotlin
interface OrdersEntryPoint {
    fun openOrder(
        orderId: String
    )
}
```

Payments does not need to know:

```text
OrdersViewModel
OrderRepositoryImpl
OrderMapper
```

---

# 231. Package Structure and Feature Encapsulation

A mature feature might look like:

```text
orders/
├── api/
│   └── OrdersEntryPoint.kt
│
├── domain/
│   ├── model/
│   ├── repository/
│   └── usecase/
│
├── data/
│   ├── remote/
│   ├── local/
│   ├── mapper/
│   └── repository/
│
├── presentation/
│   ├── list/
│   ├── details/
│   └── components/
│
└── navigation/
```

This is a strong package-level organization.

---

# 232. Package Structure and Internal Implementation

Within the feature:

```text
api
```

is public.

Everything else can be:

```text
internal
```

where appropriate.

This gives:

```text
Clear API
+
Flexible implementation
```

---

# 233. Package Structure and `internal` Across KMP

Kotlin `internal` visibility is module-scoped.

That means:

```text
commonMain
androidMain
iosMain
```

inside the same KMP module participate in the module boundary.

Use this carefully when sharing implementation details across source sets.

---

# 234. Package Structure and `internal` Platform Implementations

For example:

```kotlin
internal class AndroidSecureStorage
```

can remain inaccessible outside the module.

The public contract remains:

```kotlin
SecureStorage
```

---

# 235. Package Structure and API Leakage

Avoid returning internal implementation types from public APIs.

Bad:

```kotlin
public fun repository(): OrderRepositoryImpl
```

Better:

```kotlin
public fun repository(): OrderRepository
```

The package structure and visibility should align.

---

# 236. Package Structure and Generic Repository Types

Avoid:

```kotlin
interface Repository<T>
```

if the abstraction hides meaningful business semantics.

Prefer:

```kotlin
interface OrderRepository
```

when the repository represents a business capability.

---

# 237. Package Structure and Domain Semantics

Packages should answer:

```text
What business concept is this?
```

not merely:

```text
What technical pattern is this?
```

This is the central package-design principle.

---

# 238. Package Structure and Technical vs Semantic Organization

Technical:

```text
repositories/
mappers/
services/
models/
```

Semantic:

```text
orders/
payments/
inventory/
```

For application-level architecture, semantic organization is usually more discoverable.

---

# 239. Package Structure and Internal Technical Organization

Technical organization still has value inside a semantic boundary:

```text
orders/
├── domain/
├── data/
└── presentation/
```

The combination is powerful:

```text
Business first
Technical second
```

---

# 240. Package Structure and Vertical Slices

An alternative is to organize entirely by user journey:

```text
orders/
├── create-order/
├── cancel-order/
├── track-order/
└── order-details/
```

Each slice can contain:

```text
domain
data
presentation
```

This is useful for very large features.

---

# 241. Package Structure and Vertical Slice Example

```text
orders/
├── create/
│   ├── domain/
│   ├── data/
│   └── presentation/
│
├── cancel/
│   ├── domain/
│   └── presentation/
│
└── details/
    ├── domain/
    └── presentation/
```

This maximizes local reasoning.

---

# 242. Package Structure and Vertical Slice Trade-off

Advantages:

```text
High locality
Feature ownership
Independent evolution
```

Costs:

```text
Potential duplication
More directories
Shared domain concepts need careful placement
```

Choose based on complexity.

---

# 243. Package Structure and Hybrid Organization

A mature feature can combine:

```text
orders/
├── domain/
├── data/
├── create/
├── details/
├── cancellation/
└── navigation/
```

The structure should follow actual architecture rather than ideology.

---

# 244. Package Structure and Package-by-Change

A useful question:

> Which files usually change together?

If:

```text
OrderDetailsScreen
OrderDetailsViewModel
OrderDetailsUiState
```

change together, placing them together can reduce navigation.

---

# 245. Package Structure and Change Coupling

If:

```text
OrderDto
OrderMapper
OrderApi
```

always change together, keeping them under:

```text
data/remote
```

can be useful.

---

# 246. Package Structure and Stable Core

If:

```text
NetworkConfig
HttpClientFactory
NetworkError
```

are stable infrastructure concepts, keep them under:

```text
core/network
```

rather than duplicating them across features.

---

# 247. Package Structure and Dependency Weight

A package should not import a huge amount of unrelated code.

If:

```text
orders.domain
```

imports:

```text
Compose
Android
Firebase
Database
```

the package boundary is not protecting the domain.

---

# 248. Package Structure and Import Review

A quick review technique:

Look at imports.

For:

```text
domain
```

you should generally see:

```text
Kotlin
Shared domain abstractions
Small common libraries
```

not:

```text
Android
Compose
Vendor SDKs
UI frameworks
```

---

# 249. Package Structure and Architecture Smell

If the package name says:

```text
domain
```

but the imports say:

```text
android.*
androidx.compose.*
com.google.firebase.*
```

the architecture is lying.

Packages should reflect reality.

---

# 250. Package Structure and Architecture Honesty

Good architecture has alignment:

```text
Name
+
Location
+
Visibility
+
Dependencies
+
Ownership
```

all tell the same story.

---

# 251. Package Structure and Review Checklist

When reviewing a package:

```text
[ ] Does the package have one clear purpose?
[ ] Do classes share a reason to change?
[ ] Is ownership obvious?
[ ] Is the name semantic?
[ ] Are dependencies appropriate?
[ ] Are platform APIs isolated?
[ ] Are feature boundaries respected?
[ ] Is the package too deep?
[ ] Is it too broad?
[ ] Does it need a module instead?
```

---

# 252. Package Creation Checklist

Before creating a new package:

```text
1. What concept does it represent?
2. Who owns it?
3. What classes belong together?
4. Will the package improve discoverability?
5. Is a module actually required?
6. Is the package likely to grow?
7. Does it reflect business language?
```

---

# 253. Package Removal Checklist

Remove a package when:

```text
It contains one unrelated class
Its boundary is no longer meaningful
Its contents belong to another package
It only exists because of historical structure
It increases navigation without adding clarity
```

---

# 254. Package Refactoring Checklist

When reorganizing:

```text
[ ] Identify semantic ownership
[ ] Group related behavior
[ ] Move classes
[ ] Update imports
[ ] Review visibility
[ ] Review dependencies
[ ] Update tests
[ ] Update documentation
[ ] Check generated code
[ ] Run architecture tests
```

---

# 255. Package Decision Matrix

| Situation | Package | Module |
|---|---:|---:|
| Improve code organization | ✅ | |
| Group related classes | ✅ | |
| Create dependency boundary | | ✅ |
| Independent build boundary | | ✅ |
| Independent ownership | | ✅ |
| Hide implementation with `internal` | ✅ | |
| Public API boundary | Possible | Strong |
| Cross-team reuse | Possible | Usually better |
| Vendor isolation | Possible | Often better |
| Feature isolation | Limited | Strong |

---

# 256. Package vs Module Decision Tree

```text
Need to organize related code?
          │
         Yes
          │
          ▼
Does it need independent dependency/build isolation?
          │
      ┌───┴───┐
     No       Yes
      │         │
      ▼         ▼
   Package    Module
```

Simple, but extremely useful.

---

# 257. Package vs Module Mental Model

```text
PACKAGE
   ↓
"Where does this code belong?"

MODULE
   ↓
"Who is allowed to depend on this?"
```

This distinction prevents many architectural mistakes.

---

# 258. Package Structure and KMP Modules

A KMP module:

```text
features/orders
```

may contain:

```text
commonMain
    com.example.orders.domain
    com.example.orders.data
    com.example.orders.presentation

androidMain
    com.example.orders.platform

iosMain
    com.example.orders.platform
```

The module defines:

```text
Feature boundary
```

The packages define:

```text
Internal structure
```

---

# 259. Package Structure and Shared Modules

A shared module:

```text
core/network
```

may contain:

```text
com.example.core.network.client
com.example.core.network.config
com.example.core.network.error
```

It does not need to expose:

```text
every internal package
```

---

# 260. Package Structure and Public API

A mature shared module might expose:

```text
com.example.core.network
```

while implementation details remain:

```text
com.example.core.network.internal
```

For example:

```kotlin
public interface NetworkClient

internal class KtorNetworkClient
```

---

# 261. Package Structure and `internal` Package

An explicit:

```text
internal/
```

package can be useful for discoverability:

```text
network/
├── NetworkClient.kt
└── internal/
    ├── KtorNetworkClient.kt
    └── RetryInterceptor.kt
```

But remember:

```text
internal package name
```

does not enforce visibility.

Use:

```kotlin
internal
```

when enforcement matters.

---

# 262. Package Structure and API Package

Similarly:

```text
network/
├── api/
│   └── NetworkClient.kt
└── internal/
    ├── KtorNetworkClient.kt
    └── NetworkClientFactory.kt
```

This can make a public library easier to navigate.

---

# 263. Package Structure and Library Design

Published KMP libraries benefit from deliberate packages:

```text
library/
├── api/
├── model/
└── internal/
```

Consumers should not need to depend on internal implementation packages.

---

# 264. Package Structure and API Evolution

When implementation changes:

```text
internal
```

can change freely.

The stable API:

```text
api
```

should change deliberately.

This reduces compatibility cost.

---

# 265. Package Structure and Documentation

A package-level README can be useful for large areas:

```text
orders/domain/README.md
```

describing:

```text
Business responsibility
Dependencies
Non-goals
Examples
```

Do not add READMEs everywhere.

Use them where complexity warrants.

---

# 266. Package Structure and Architecture Diagrams

A package diagram might show:

```text
orders
│
├── presentation
│       │
│       ▼
├── domain ◄──────── data
│
└── navigation
```

This is more useful than a diagram showing every class.

---

# 267. Package Structure and Diagram Granularity

Architecture diagrams should match the level being discussed.

```text
Repository diagram
→ Modules

Feature diagram
→ Packages

Domain diagram
→ Classes
```

Do not mix all levels into one unreadable diagram.

---

# 268. Package Structure and Documentation Levels

A good documentation hierarchy:

```text
README
  ↓
Module documentation
  ↓
Package documentation
  ↓
Class documentation
```

Each level explains only what is necessary.

---

# 269. Package Structure and Onboarding

A new engineer can navigate:

```text
features/orders
```

and discover:

```text
domain
data
presentation
navigation
```

without needing to understand the entire repository.

This is a major productivity benefit.

---

# 270. Package Structure and Cognitive Load

Good packages reduce:

```text
Search space
Dependency uncertainty
Ownership ambiguity
```

Bad packages increase:

```text
"Where is this?"
"Who owns this?"
"Can I use this?"
"Why does this depend on that?"
```

---

# 271. Package Structure and Architecture Quality

Package quality can be evaluated by:

```text
Discoverability
Cohesion
Semantic naming
Dependency clarity
Ownership clarity
Change locality
```

not by:

```text
Number of packages
```

---

# 272. Package Structure and Module Quality

Likewise, module quality is not:

```text
Number of modules
```

It is:

```text
Meaningful boundaries
+
Controlled dependencies
+
Build isolation
+
Ownership
```

Packages and modules solve different problems.

---

# 273. Package Structure and the DRY Trap

Do not force identical code into one package just to satisfy:

```text
DRY
```

DRY should reduce harmful duplication.

It should not create semantic coupling.

---

# 274. Package Structure and Reuse Decision

Ask:

```text
Is the behavior reusable?
```

Then:

```text
Is the meaning reusable?
```

Then:

```text
Is the ownership reusable?
```

Only then consider sharing.

---

# 275. Package Structure and Copy-Paste

A small amount of duplication can preserve:

```text
Feature autonomy
```

For example:

```text
OrderStatusUiModel
PaymentStatusUiModel
```

may intentionally be separate even if their structures look similar.

---

# 276. Package Structure and Bounded Contexts

Packages are excellent for representing bounded contexts:

```text
orders/
payments/
inventory/
identity/
```

Each context can define its own:

```text
Models
Policies
Services
Errors
Events
```

without creating a global model namespace.

---

# 277. Package Structure and Context-Specific Models

For example:

```text
orders/domain/model/Customer.kt
payments/domain/model/BillingCustomer.kt
profile/domain/model/CustomerProfile.kt
```

These names can coexist because package context provides meaning.

---

# 278. Package Structure and Model Name Collisions

Two classes can both be named:

```text
Customer
```

if their packages differ:

```text
orders.domain.model.Customer
payments.domain.model.Customer
```

This is not necessarily a problem.

The package communicates context.

---

# 279. Package Structure and Imports

If name collisions become frequent:

```kotlin
import orders.domain.model.Customer
import payments.domain.model.Customer
```

consider whether:

```text
Customer
```

is actually too generic.

Better domain names can reduce ambiguity:

```text
OrderCustomer
BillingCustomer
```

when appropriate.

---

# 280. Package Structure and Package Aliases

Kotlin supports import aliases:

```kotlin
import orders.domain.model.Customer as OrderCustomer
```

This can be useful, but should not be a substitute for clear domain modeling.

---

# 281. Package Structure and Shared Domain

A genuinely shared value object:

```text
core/domain/money
```

can be imported by multiple contexts.

But do not create:

```text
core/domain
```

containing every domain model in the application.

---

# 282. Package Structure and Core Domain

The word:

```text
core
```

must remain meaningful.

A healthy Core might contain:

```text
network
security
database
observability
time
concurrency
```

plus a small number of truly shared domain primitives.

---

# 283. Package Structure and Core Package Growth

Watch:

```text
core/
├── models/
├── utils/
├── helpers/
├── services/
├── managers/
└── business/
```

This is usually a sign that Core has become a dumping ground.

Split by capability.

---

# 284. Package Structure and Shared Module Package Tree

Good:

```text
core/
├── network/
│   ├── client/
│   ├── config/
│   └── error/
│
├── security/
│   ├── auth/
│   ├── storage/
│   └── crypto/
│
└── observability/
    ├── logging/
    ├── analytics/
    └── metrics/
```

Each package communicates a responsibility.

---

# 285. Package Structure and Feature Package Tree

Good:

```text
features/orders/
├── api/
├── domain/
│   ├── model/
│   ├── repository/
│   └── policy/
├── data/
│   ├── remote/
│   ├── local/
│   ├── mapper/
│   └── repository/
├── presentation/
│   ├── list/
│   ├── details/
│   └── components/
└── navigation/
```

This is readable without being excessively granular.

---

# 286. Package Structure and Large Feature Tree

For a very large feature:

```text
orders/
├── create/
│   ├── domain/
│   ├── data/
│   └── presentation/
│
├── cancellation/
│   ├── domain/
│   └── presentation/
│
├── fulfillment/
│   ├── domain/
│   ├── data/
│   └── presentation/
│
└── tracking/
    ├── domain/
    ├── data/
    └── presentation/
```

This can be a better structure than one giant `orders/domain`.

---

# 287. Package Structure and Complexity-Driven Design

There is no universal:

```text
correct package tree
```

The correct structure depends on:

```text
Feature complexity
Team size
Change frequency
Platform count
Reuse
Ownership
Build boundaries
```

---

# 288. Package Structure and Small KMP App

For a small app:

```text
shared/
└── src/commonMain/
    └── com/example/app/
        ├── data/
        ├── domain/
        └── presentation/
```

This may be enough.

Do not introduce:

```text
30 feature modules
```

for a simple application.

---

# 289. Package Structure and Medium KMP App

For a medium application:

```text
features/
├── orders/
├── payments/
└── profile/

core/
├── network/
├── database/
└── security/
```

Each module has semantic packages.

---

# 290. Package Structure and Large KMP App

For a large enterprise application:

```text
apps/
features/
core/
platform/
contracts/
integrations/
design-system/
testing/
build-logic/
```

Inside each:

```text
Semantic packages
```

provide internal organization.

---

# 291. Package Structure and Enterprise Mental Model

Think:

```text
Repository
   │
   ▼
Modules
   │
   ▼
Business / Capability Boundaries
   │
   ▼
Packages
   │
   ▼
Implementation
```

Modules answer:

```text
Who depends on whom?
```

Packages answer:

```text
Where does this behavior belong?
```

---

# 292. Package Structure and Architecture Governance

A mature organization can define package conventions:

```text
feature/
    api/
    domain/
    data/
    presentation/
    navigation/

core/
    capability-specific packages

platform/
    capability-specific packages
```

These conventions improve consistency.

But conventions should remain guidelines rather than forcing unnecessary structure.

---

# 293. Package Structure and Automated Rules

Useful architecture checks can enforce:

```text
domain cannot import presentation
domain cannot import Android SDK
core cannot import features
features cannot import internal packages of other features
platform implementations cannot leak into commonMain
```

Packages provide the vocabulary.

Tools provide enforcement.

---

# 294. Package Structure and Import Boundaries

Example rule:

```text
com.example.orders.domain
```

may depend on:

```text
com.example.orders.domain.*
com.example.core.domain.*
```

but not:

```text
com.example.orders.presentation.*
android.*
androidx.compose.*
```

The exact allowed graph depends on the architecture.

---

# 295. Package Structure and Architecture Tests

Architecture tests should validate:

```text
Intent
```

not every file path.

For example:

```text
Domain must not depend on Presentation.
```

This remains useful even if packages are reorganized.

---

# 296. Package Structure and Refactoring Safety

When architecture rules are automated:

```text
Package refactoring
```

becomes safer because the build can tell you when a dependency rule is violated.

---

# 297. Package Structure and Static Analysis

Static analysis can detect:

```text
Cycles
Forbidden dependencies
Android API usage
Unused public APIs
Visibility issues
```

This helps maintain package architecture at scale.

---

# 298. Package Structure and Dependency Cycles

A package cycle:

```text
orders.domain
   ↓
orders.data
   ↓
orders.domain
```

may be acceptable in some implementations because data implements domain interfaces.

But:

```text
domain → data
```

is generally a dependency direction warning in Clean Architecture.

Use interfaces to invert the dependency where needed.

---

# 299. Package Structure and Dependency Inversion

```text
domain
   │
   └── OrderRepository
            ▲
            │
         data
   └── OrderRepositoryImpl
```

The package layout makes this relationship understandable.

---

# 300. Package Structure and Data Mapping

```text
data.remote
      │
      ▼
data.mapper
      │
      ▼
domain.model
```

The mapper is the translation boundary.

---

# 301. Package Structure and UI Mapping

Similarly:

```text
domain.model
      │
      ▼
presentation.mapper
      │
      ▼
presentation.model
```

when UI models provide value.

Do not introduce UI models automatically if the domain model is already an appropriate UI contract.

---

# 302. Package Structure and Simplicity

Architecture should not become:

```text
Domain
→ Mapper
→ DTO
→ Entity
→ Repository
→ Adapter
→ Wrapper
→ Facade
```

for every single property.

Introduce boundaries when they solve real problems.

---

# 303. Package Structure and Practicality

A useful principle:

> **The package tree should make the common path obvious.**

If developers need to open:

```text
12 packages
```

to understand one simple feature, the structure may be too fragmented.

---

# 304. Package Structure and Navigation Cost

More packages mean:

```text
More navigation
```

Fewer packages mean:

```text
Potentially lower organization
```

The goal is the right balance.

---

# 305. Package Structure and Module Economics

Think about:

```text
Module cost
Package cost
Cognitive cost
Build cost
Maintenance cost
```

A package is cheap.

A Gradle module is more expensive.

Use the stronger boundary only when the value justifies it.

---

# 306. Package Structure and Gradle Boundaries

If the main reason is:

```text
"These classes look related."
```

use a package.

If the reason is:

```text
"These classes must not depend on each other."
```

consider a module.

---

# 307. Package Structure and API Exposure

A package can organize:

```text
Public
Internal
```

but module boundaries provide stronger control.

For reusable shared libraries:

```text
Module + `internal`
```

is usually more robust than package naming alone.

---

# 308. Package Structure and KMP Libraries

A KMP library might expose:

```text
com.company.analytics.Analytics
```

while hiding:

```text
com.company.analytics.internal.*
```

The package structure is part of the library's user experience.

---

# 309. Package Structure and Semantic Versioning

Once a package becomes part of a published API:

```text
Package name
+
Class name
+
Function signature
```

may become compatibility commitments.

Be deliberate about what becomes public.

---

# 310. Package Structure and API Stability

The more external consumers:

```text
More important
```

it becomes to maintain stable package names.

Internal application packages can be refactored more freely.

---

# 311. Package Structure and Migration

If package names must change:

```text
Old package
   ↓
Deprecated compatibility layer
   ↓
New package
```

may help published libraries.

For internal applications, direct migration is often simpler.

---

# 312. Package Structure and Documentation Comments

Public APIs should have useful documentation:

```kotlin
/**
 * Provides secure application-level credential storage.
 */
interface SecureStorage
```

Package structure tells users:

```text
Where
```

documentation tells them:

```text
What
```

---

# 313. Package Structure and Discoverability

A developer should be able to predict the package before searching.

For example:

```text
SecureStorage
→ core.security.storage

OrderCancellationPolicy
→ orders.domain.policy

OrderDto
→ orders.data.remote
```

Predictability is a sign of good architecture.

---

# 314. Package Structure and Consistency

If one feature uses:

```text
domain/
data/
presentation/
```

and another uses:

```text
business/
repository/
ui/
```

without a reason, developers must learn two conventions.

Consistency reduces cognitive load.

---

# 315. Package Structure and Exceptions

Consistency should not become dogma.

If a feature is tiny:

```text
orders/
├── Order.kt
└── OrderRepository.kt
```

may be better than:

```text
orders/
├── domain/
│   ├── model/
│   └── repository/
```

Architecture should serve the code.

---

# 316. Package Structure and Evolution

Start simple:

```text
orders/
```

Add structure when complexity appears:

```text
orders/
├── domain/
└── data/
```

Then:

```text
orders/
├── domain/
│   ├── model/
│   └── policy/
└── data/
    ├── remote/
    └── local/
```

This is healthy incremental architecture.

---

# 317. Package Structure and Refactoring Trigger

Split a package when:

```text
It becomes difficult to navigate
Classes have different owners
Classes change for unrelated reasons
Dependencies become confusing
Subdomain boundaries emerge
```

Do not split simply because a package has:

```text
20 files
```

File count alone is not enough.

---

# 318. Package Structure and Merge Conflicts

Good package ownership can reduce merge conflicts because teams work in:

```text
Separate feature directories
```

rather than:

```text
Global services/
Global models/
Global managers/
```

---

# 319. Package Structure and Parallel Development

```text
Orders Team
└── features/orders

Payments Team
└── features/payments

Platform Team
└── core/network
```

Teams can work in parallel with fewer accidental collisions.

---

# 320. Package Structure and Team Autonomy

A good package structure supports:

```text
Independent ownership
Local testing
Local reasoning
Targeted reviews
```

This is particularly valuable in enterprise KMP.

---

# 321. Package Structure and Code Review Routing

Code review can be automatically routed based on:

```text
Package path
```

For example:

```text
features/orders/**
```

goes to:

```text
Orders Team
```

This aligns repository structure with organizational structure.

---

# 322. Package Structure and On-Call

During incidents:

```text
Package path
```

can help identify:

```text
Owning team
Relevant subsystem
Likely dependencies
```

This is another reason semantic organization matters.

---

# 323. Package Structure and Architecture Communication

A package tree can often explain architecture without a diagram.

For example:

```text
orders/
├── domain/
├── data/
└── presentation/
```

immediately communicates:

```text
Business
Persistence
UI
```

---

# 324. Package Structure and the "Open the Folder" Test

A practical test:

> Open the feature directory and look at it for five seconds.

Can you answer:

```text
What does this feature own?
Where is business logic?
Where is data?
Where is UI?
```

If yes, the package structure is doing its job.

---

# 325. Package Structure and the "Where Should I Put This?" Test

A developer adds:

```text
OrderCancellationPolicy
```

If the architecture makes the answer obvious:

```text
orders/domain/policy
```

the structure is healthy.

If the team debates:

```text
common?
utils?
services?
managers?
```

the architecture needs clearer ownership.

---

# 326. Package Structure and the "Can I Import This?" Test

A developer sees:

```text
orders.internal.OrderRepositoryImpl
```

and asks:

> Can I use this?

If the answer is unclear, visibility and API structure need improvement.

---

# 327. Package Structure and the "Why Does This Exist?" Test

Every package should survive:

```text
Why does this package exist?
```

A good answer is:

```text
It groups Order cancellation policies.
```

A weak answer is:

```text
It contains things used by multiple classes.
```

---

# 328. Package Structure and the "Who Owns This?" Test

A good answer:

```text
Orders Team
```

or:

```text
Platform Team
```

A weak answer:

```text
Everyone
```

Shared ownership should be explicit.

---

# 329. Package Structure and the "What Changes Together?" Test

If classes usually change together:

```text
Same package
```

may make sense.

If they change independently:

```text
Separate packages
```

may be better.

This is one of the most useful package design heuristics.

---

# 330. Package Structure and the "What Depends on What?" Test

Package structure should make dependencies understandable:

```text
presentation → domain
data → domain
```

If everything imports everything:

```text
Package organization is not enough.
```

The module architecture needs improvement.

---

# 331. Package Structure and the "What Is Shared?" Test

A shared package should have:

```text
Explicit purpose
```

not:

```text
Accidental reuse
```

If code is shared because:

```text
"It was already there"
```

re-evaluate the boundary.

---

# 332. Package Structure and KMP Architecture

The complete KMP hierarchy can be visualized:

```text
                       REPOSITORY
                           │
              ┌────────────┼────────────┐
              ▼            ▼            ▼
             APP        FEATURES       CORE
                           │            │
                           ▼            ▼
                       PACKAGES      PACKAGES
                           │            │
                    ┌──────┼──────┐     │
                    ▼      ▼      ▼     ▼
                  DOMAIN  DATA  UI   PLATFORM
                    │      │      │
                    └──────┴──────┘
                           │
                         TYPES
```

Each layer solves a different problem.

---

# 333. The Package Mental Model

Remember:

```text
MODULE
=
Who can depend on this?

PACKAGE
=
Where does this code belong?

CLASS
=
What behavior does it provide?
```

This simple model prevents overengineering.

---

# 334. Package Design Golden Rules

```text
1. Organize around meaning.
2. Prefer feature-first organization for application code.
3. Use packages for logical organization.
4. Use modules for dependency and build boundaries.
5. Keep domain, data, and presentation concepts distinguishable.
6. Keep platform implementations isolated.
7. Use `internal` to enforce module-level implementation boundaries.
8. Avoid global `models`, `utils`, `helpers`, and `managers`.
9. Keep feature-specific behavior near the feature.
10. Keep shared capabilities in semantic shared modules.
11. Organize UI by feature/screen when that improves locality.
12. Keep DTOs near their data boundary.
13. Keep UI models near presentation.
14. Keep domain models near domain ownership.
15. Do not create packages only because a pattern says so.
16. Let package depth follow complexity.
17. Use package boundaries to improve discoverability.
18. Use modules or architecture tests when actual enforcement is required.
19. Prefer stable package names for public libraries.
20. Optimize for local reasoning and controlled change.
```

---

# 335. Package Design Checklist

Before committing a package structure:

```text
[ ] Does every package have a clear purpose?
[ ] Does the name describe meaning?
[ ] Is ownership obvious?
[ ] Are related classes grouped?
[ ] Are unrelated classes separated?
[ ] Is package depth reasonable?
[ ] Are platform APIs isolated?
[ ] Are dependencies directional?
[ ] Are public APIs intentional?
[ ] Are internal details protected?
[ ] Is a Gradle module actually needed?
[ ] Does the structure support local reasoning?
[ ] Does it support future modularization?
[ ] Does it reduce navigation cost?
[ ] Does it avoid generic dumping grounds?
```

---

# 336. Common Mistakes

## Mistake 1 — Global `models`

```text
models/
├── User
├── Order
├── Payment
└── Product
```

### Why it fails

It hides business ownership and creates coupling.

### Better

```text
orders/domain/model
payments/domain/model
profile/domain/model
```

or intentionally shared contracts.

---

## Mistake 2 — Global `utils`

```text
utils/
```

### Why it fails

Everything can be placed there.

### Better

Use semantic packages:

```text
core/time
core/localization
orders/domain
```

---

## Mistake 3 — Global `services`

```text
services/
```

### Why it fails

It groups unrelated business capabilities.

### Better

```text
orders/domain
payments/domain
core/network
```

depending on responsibility.

---

## Mistake 4 — Global `repositories`

```text
repositories/
```

### Why it fails

It separates repository code from its business owner.

### Better

```text
orders/domain/repository
orders/data/repository
```

---

## Mistake 5 — Global `viewmodels`

```text
viewmodels/
```

### Why it fails

UI behavior becomes disconnected from features.

### Better

```text
orders/presentation/order-details
```

---

## Mistake 6 — Package Naming Used as Enforcement

```text
internal/
```

does not automatically make code internal.

### Better

Use:

```kotlin
internal
```

for actual visibility enforcement.

---

## Mistake 7 — Too Many Packages

```text
order/
├── domain/
├── domain-model/
├── domain-model-value/
├── domain-model-value-object/
└── ...
```

### Why it fails

Navigation becomes harder than the original problem.

### Better

Introduce packages when semantic boundaries actually appear.

---

## Mistake 8 — Too Few Packages

```text
orders/
└── 300 Kotlin files
```

### Why it fails

Ownership and responsibility become difficult to discover.

### Better

Split by meaningful concepts:

```text
domain
data
presentation
navigation
subdomains
```

---

## Mistake 9 — Platform Leakage

```text
orders/domain
└── AndroidContext.kt
```

### Why it fails

Common business code becomes platform-dependent.

### Better

Use:

```text
platform capability
```

or:

```text
expect / actual
```

when appropriate.

---

## Mistake 10 — Cross-Feature Internal Imports

```text
payments
   ↓
orders.internal
```

### Why it fails

Feature encapsulation is broken.

### Better

Expose a small feature API or shared contract.

---

## Mistake 11 — Global UI Components

```text
components/
├── OrderCard
├── PaymentCard
├── ProfileHeader
```

### Why it fails

Business-specific UI gets mixed together.

### Better

```text
orders/presentation
payments/presentation
profile/presentation
```

and keep generic visual primitives in the design system.

---

## Mistake 12 — Global Events

```text
events/
├── OrderCreated
├── PaymentCompleted
├── ProfileUpdated
```

### Why it fails

It can become hidden coupling.

### Better

Define event ownership and semantics first.

---

## Mistake 13 — Package Names That Describe Technology Only

```text
processors/
handlers/
managers/
```

### Why it fails

They hide business meaning.

### Better

Use:

```text
orders/cancellation
payments/authorization
inventory/replenishment
```

where appropriate.

---

## Mistake 14 — Treating Package Count as Architecture Quality

```text
500 packages = mature architecture
```

False.

Quality comes from:

```text
Cohesion
+
Coupling
+
Ownership
+
Change isolation
```

---

## Mistake 15 — Creating Modules When Packages Are Enough

If two classes only need logical grouping:

```text
Use a package.
```

Do not create:

```text
Gradle module
```

without a meaningful boundary.

---

# 337. A Practical Package Design Workflow

When adding new code:

```text
Step 1
Identify the business/capability owner.

Step 2
Identify whether it is:
- Domain
- Data
- Presentation
- Infrastructure
- Platform
- Contract

Step 3
Find the nearest semantic package.

Step 4
Check whether the package already has the same reason to change.

Step 5
If not, create a new package.

Step 6
If the code needs a dependency boundary, consider a module.

Step 7
Protect implementation with `internal`.

Step 8
Validate dependency direction.

Step 9
Add tests close to the package.

Step 10
Document unusual architectural decisions.
```

---

# 338. Example: Adding Order Cancellation

Requirement:

```text
Add order cancellation.
```

Start with ownership:

```text
Orders
```

Then responsibility:

```text
Business rule
```

Package:

```text
orders/domain/cancellation
```

Possible files:

```text
CancellationPolicy.kt
CancelOrder.kt
CancellationReason.kt
```

Presentation:

```text
orders/presentation/order-details/
```

Data:

```text
orders/data/remote/
```

The package structure follows the capability.

---

# 339. Example: Adding Secure Storage

Requirement:

```text
Store authentication token securely.
```

Ownership:

```text
Security infrastructure
```

Package:

```text
core/security/storage
```

KMP source sets:

```text
commonMain
androidMain
iosMain
```

Platform implementation:

```text
Android → Keystore
iOS → Keychain
```

The package communicates:

```text
Capability
```

not:

```text
Vendor implementation
```

---

# 340. Example: Adding Analytics

Requirement:

```text
Track order completed.
```

Generic mechanism:

```text
core/observability/analytics
```

Feature event:

```text
orders/analytics/OrderCompleted.kt
```

Vendor adapter:

```text
integrations/analytics/FirebaseAnalyticsAdapter.kt
```

This gives three clear responsibilities:

```text
Feature meaning
Shared capability
Vendor implementation
```

---

# 341. Example: Adding a Button

Requirement:

```text
Add a reusable button.
```

If it is:

```text
Generic visual component
```

place it in:

```text
design-system/button
```

If it is:

```text
Cancel Order business UI
```

place it in:

```text
orders/presentation
```

The package depends on semantic ownership.

---

# 342. Example: Adding a Date Formatter

Requirement:

```text
Format an ISO date.
```

If generic:

```text
core/localization/date
```

may be appropriate.

If:

```text
Format order delivery date
```

keep it near:

```text
orders
```

The same technical operation can have different architectural ownership.

---

# 343. Example: Adding a Retry Policy

Requirement:

```text
Retry failed network requests.
```

Generic mechanism:

```text
core/network/retry
```

Business decision:

```text
Should payment authorization be retried?
```

belongs to:

```text
payments
```

Infrastructure provides the mechanism.

Business owns the policy.

---

# 344. Final KMP Package Architecture

A mature KMP project can be understood as:

```text
                         KMP REPOSITORY
                               │
        ┌──────────────────────┼──────────────────────┐
        ▼                      ▼                      ▼
      APPS                  FEATURES                  CORE
        │                      │                      │
        │             ┌────────┼────────┐       ┌─────┼─────┐
        │             ▼        ▼        ▼       ▼     ▼     ▼
        │           Orders   Payments  Profile  Net  Sec   DB
        │             │        │        │
        │             └────────┼────────┘
        │                      │
        │                 PACKAGES
        │                      │
        │        ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
        │        ▼             ▼             ▼
        │      DOMAIN         DATA      PRESENTATION
        │        │             │             │
        │        ▼             ▼             ▼
        │     Business      Sources         UI
        │      Rules        & Mapping      State
        │
        └─────────────────────────────────────────────
```

The important hierarchy is:

```text
Repository
    ↓
Module
    ↓
Feature / Capability
    ↓
Package
    ↓
Type
```

And the responsibility of each level is different:

```text
Module
→ Controls dependency/build boundaries

Feature
→ Owns business capability

Package
→ Organizes related implementation

Class
→ Implements behavior
```

---

# 345. The Final Mental Model

When deciding where code belongs, ask these questions in order:

```text
1. What does this code mean?
             ↓
2. Who owns that meaning?
             ↓
3. Is it feature-specific or shared?
             ↓
4. Which module should own it?
             ↓
5. Which package best represents its responsibility?
             ↓
6. What should be public?
             ↓
7. What should remain internal?
             ↓
8. What dependencies are allowed?
```

This prevents the common mistake of starting with:

```text
"What folder should I put this file in?"
```

Architecture should begin with:

```text
"What does this code belong to?"
```

---

# 346. Final Takeaways

Packages are not merely folders.

They are part of the architecture's **language**.

A good KMP package structure makes:

```text
Business ownership
Dependency direction
Platform boundaries
Feature boundaries
Code discoverability
Change locality
```

visible to developers.

The strongest package architecture usually follows:

```text
                  BUSINESS
                     │
                     ▼
                  FEATURE
                     │
                     ▼
                  PACKAGE
                     │
          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
          ▼          ▼          ▼
       DOMAIN       DATA   PRESENTATION
          │          │          │
          ▼          ▼          ▼
       Rules      Sources       UI
```

while shared infrastructure remains outside the feature:

```text
FEATURE
   │
   ▼
SHARED CAPABILITY
   │
   ▼
PLATFORM
   │
   ▼
VENDOR
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Use modules to control dependencies. Use packages to organize meaning. Use visibility to protect implementation.**

And the most important rule for KMP project structure is:

> **Do not organize code merely by what it technically is. Organize it by what it means, who owns it, and how it changes.**

That is what turns a package tree into an architectural map rather than just a collection of folders.
